// Petal Quest — artwork.
//
// Pure data: every function here returns string art and nothing else, so this
// module imports nothing and reaches no native. `game.ptl` owns where the
// tiles land in the pattern table and which palette each is drawn with.
//
// Two conventions run through the whole file:
//
//  1. **Digits are palette entries, not colors.** `0`/`.` is entry 0 — the
//     backdrop for a background tile, transparent for a sprite — and 1/2/3 are
//     the sub-palette's three real colors, darkest to brightest. The same art
//     therefore reads as stone or as marble depending only on which palette
//     `game.ptl` draws it with, which is what makes three tilesets out of one
//     set of shapes affordable.
//  2. **The three tilesets share one slot layout.** `tileset(n)` always
//     returns the same 16 entries in the same order (see `T_*` in game.ptl),
//     so the level legend, the solid-tile range and the column painter are
//     written once and never mention which world they are in. This is CHR-bank
//     swapping, and it is the reason a level change costs 16 tile writes.
//
// Sprites are authored as one 16x16 (or 32x32) grid and sliced into tiles by
// `define_meta`, so what is written here is what appears on screen.

// ── Background tilesets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Slot order, identical for all three worlds:
//   0 empty      1 ground-top   2 ground-fill  3 brick
//   4 hard block 5 ledge-left   6 ledge-mid    7 ledge-right
//   8 deco-A     9 deco-B      10 tuft        11 cloud-left
//  12 cloud-right 13 pole       14 spikes      15 banner
// Slots 1-7 are the solid ones; 14 hurts; everything else is scenery.

